<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Padishah Khatun</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1256">1256</a>–<a href="/years/1295">1295</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Padishah Khatun was a ruler of the Qutlugh-Khanid dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerman">Kerman</a>, in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, and a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qutlugh-terken-khatun">Qutlugh Terken Khatun</a>. She married the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhan">Ilkhan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abaqa-khan">Abaqa</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaykhatu">Gaykhatu</a>, and governed Kerman from 1292. After Gaykhatu's fall she was deposed and executed in 1295. She was also known as a poet.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/padishah-khatun.jpg" alt="Padishah Khatun" width="320" height="443" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Elmi Publishing Institute · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Padishah_Khatun_-_1956_drawing.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Padishah Khatun, whose name was Safwat al-Din Khatun, was born in 1256, the youngest daughter of Qutb al-Din, who died in 1257, and Kutlugh Turkan. She held her own <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a> of Sirjan after her mother attended the coronation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abaqa-khan">Abaqa</a> in 1265, and on 22 May 1272 she married Abaqa Khan, a match her mother arranged to secure Mongol support. She converted from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> on her marriage and was granted the household of Abaqa's late mother Yesunjin. She backed her mother against her siblings Muzaffar al-Din Hajjaj in 1276 and Suyurghatmish in 1280.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abaqa-khan">Abaqa</a>'s death in 1282 she stayed at court with her mother, who died in 1283, and sent her sister Bibi Khatun to guard her interests in Kirman. Through her influence on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arghun">Arghun</a> she won a share in the rule of Kirman in 1284, but the vizier Buqa favoured Suyurghatmish, had her married to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaykhatu">Gaykhatu</a> and removed her to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> in 1286. When Gaykhatu was elected in 1291, she asked for Kirman as her personal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a>, and he agreed.</p><p>She imprisoned her half-brother Suyurghatmish in October 1292; he escaped with the help of his wife Kurdujin Khatun, was imprisoned again and was strangled on 21 August 1294. She was granted the rule of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yazd">Yazd</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shabankara">Shabankara</a> as well, and in Ormus replaced the prince Rukn al-Din Masud with Sayf al-Din Ayaz. Her coins struck in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerman">Kerman</a> named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaykhatu">Gaykhatu</a> as overlord with the title Padishah-I Jahan, ruler of the world.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaykhatu">Gaykhatu</a> was assassinated on 21 March 1295, she was imprisoned on the orders of Kurdujin Khatun and Shah Alam, Suyurghatmish's widow and daughter, and strangled on her way to Baydu's court at Kushk-e Zar in June or July 1295. She was buried in the Gonbad-e Sabz Mausoleum. Marco Polo described her as an ambitious and clever woman who put her own brother to death as a rival. She wrote Persian poetry under the pseudonyms Lala Khatun and Hasanshah and was skilled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calligraphy">calligraphy</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Padishah Khatun” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Her daughter <b>Padishah Khatun</b>, wife successively of the Ilkhans Abaqa and Gaykhatu, ruled Kerman from 1292 but was executed in 1295.</blockquote><a href="/state/qutlugh-khanid-dynasty">Qutlugh-Khanid dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
